"MBH Safety Filter Draft (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.12083v1)\nciteturn2839view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 30; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.12083v1\",\"lineno\":125}); Total lines: 1079\nL115@P6: specifically 794 messages (397 crisis, 397 non-crisis) that were human data, as the NVIDIA\nL116@P6: dataset, focusing on \"Suicide and Self Harm,\" excluding samples from the Suicide Detection\nL117@P6: dataset and duplicate rows. Two licensed clinicians (BWN, JT) independently reviewed\nL118@P6: messages, reclassifying 6.927% of the total (26 \"safe\" to \"unsafe,\" 29 \"unsafe\" to \"safe\") to\nL119@P6: ensure label accuracy.\nL120@P6: Safety Filters, Content Moderation, and Guardrail Models.\nL121@P6: Verily behavioral health safety filter (VBHSF). The VBHSF is a transformer-based\nL122@P6: LLM (GPT architecture) that uses advanced prompt engineering and clinical reasoning to detect\nL123@P6: the presence of a crisis within a message and classify the specific crisis type.\nL124@P6: Open Source Safety Guardrails. We selected two open-source content moderation\nL125@P6: guardrails, with specific categories that included safety and mental health. The first, OpenAI's\nL126@P6: omni-moderation-latest model21,24, built on GPT-4o, automatically flags text and images for\nL127@P6: policy violations across 13 predefined categories and returns a binary flag and JSON output. The\nL128@P6: second, nvidia/llama-3.1-nemoguard-8b-content-safety20,23is an open-source model that\nL129@P6-7: classifies content across 23 risk categories and outputs a JSON.Statistical Analysis\nL130@P7: All statistical analyses were conducted using Python (version 3.9.6). The analyses were\nL131@P7: structured to evaluate the internal performance of the VBHSF, followed by evaluation with the\nL132@P7: NVIDIA dataset, and against external guardrails.\nL133@P7: The internal evaluation assessed the VBHSF on the Verily dataset. This included the\nL134@P7: performance of the Stage 1 classifier for overall crisis detection, and the performance of the\nL135@P7: Stage 2 multi-label classifier on messages flagged as \"Crisis\" by the Stage 1 model. The Stage 2\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAnalyzing and Internalizing Complex Policy Documents for LLM Agents (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11588v1)\nciteturn2839view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11588v1\",\"lineno\":150}); Total lines: 523\nL150: As shown in the upper part of Figure cite76†2 , our pipeline begins with an LLM-based preprocessing step: the LLM is prompted to identify task types in the policy, extract the corresponding specifications, and classify them into these four categories. In parallel, the LLM determines the valid scope of each specification to construct a complete representation of the policy. For more complex cases in practice, this process may be enhanced by an optional manual check to ensure the categorization is accurate.\nL151: #### Targeted Continued Pretraining Data Generation\nL152: After policy analysis and categorization, our pipeline leverages an LLM to generate targeted data for each specification type. In all cases, direct references to the policy are replaced with the policy identifier $pid$. As illustrated in Figure cite76†2 , we adopt a “targeted therapy” perspective: the data generation process is tailored to the distinct complexity of each specification category. For factual specifications, the primary challenge is memorization and accurate recall.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSelf-Verifying ReflectionHelps Transformers with CoT Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12157v1)\nciteturn2839view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12157v